{"answer_type": "FREE_TEXT", "documents": ["rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001"], "family": "c", "gold": {"answer": "HART 4-20 mA: if self-diagnostics detect a sensor or microprocessor failure, the output is driven high or low; the direction is user-selectable with a jumper/switch on the transmitter. Factory default is High alarm unless specified (CT = Rosemount standard low alarm, CN = NAMUR low, C4 = NAMUR high). Levels: default high >= 21.75 mA (>= 22.5 mA with M6, DA1, T9, RK), low <= 3.75 mA; NAMUR >= 22.5 / <= 3.6 mA; custom 20.2-23.0 / 3.6-3.8 mA. Output M: below 0.94 V or above 5.4 V, selectable by internal jumper. F, W, X: failure passed as alert and status.", "answerable": "yes", "category": "conditional", "must": ["high or low, user-selectable by jumper/switch", "default high", "alarm values per standard/NAMUR/custom", "output M internal jumper"], "relation": "fed_by_transmitter", "via_tag": "FT-10-1"}, "qid": "c01", "source": {"file": "docs\\private\\30-experiments\\EXP-0039-rosemount-kerdesek\\rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001.json", "kind": "plantdocs", "question_id": "rm-v30", "step": {"anchor": "FIC-10-1", "relation": "fed_by_transmitter", "target": "FT-10-1", "target_document": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001"}}, "split": "dev", "tags": ["FIC-10-1", "FT-10-1"], "text": "Consider the transmitter that feeds controller FIC-10-1. When the transmitter fails, does the output go high or low, and how do I change that alarm direction?"}
